{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Firelight and Rust: Endeavour Segment Snowblower Vent",
    "date": "2026-09-26",
    "lesson": "At the Endeavour hydrothermal segment on the Juan de Fuca Ridge, some vent orifices erupt cloudy white 'snowblower' plumes of neutrally buoyant filamentous sulfur bacteria before a new chimney forms. Here, chemolithotrophic sulfur-oxidizers fix carbon from dissolved hydrogen sulfide with no light at all, living in a thin plume band close to the diffuse-flow rock. Their neighbours are iron-oxidizers rusting the basalt itself and heterotrophs scavenging the biomass that constantly sloughs off both. Because the plume is patchy and short-lived, cells that sense and track sulfide with chemotaxis outcompete ones that just swim randomly, and EPS matters more than usual: it is what lets a filament anchor to rock instead of being swept off into the abyssal cold beyond the plume edge, where there is no energy at all.",
    "citation": "Juan de Fuca Ridge Endeavour segment 'snowblower' vent observations (Woods Hole/NOAA VENTS program)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Endeavour segment snowblower vents, Juan de Fuca Ridge, ~2200m depth"
  },
